The recording was highlighted by <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/09/iphone_4_antennagate_q_and_a"><em>Daring Fireball</em>'s John Gruber</a>, who attended the event. Apple called the July 16, 2010 press conference amid complaints that holding the iPhone 4 in a particular way could reduce its cellular signal.<br/>
<br/>
Jobs used his prepared presentation to announce free cases for eligible customers and a 30-day return option. Apple later posted video of that presentation, but <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2010/07/16/apple-posts-video-of-iphone-4-press-conference-antenna-information-pages/">left out the Q&A</a>, which was documented at the time through written reports and photographs.<br/>
<br/>
Among the exchanges now visible is a question from Gruber at about 18:45 asking whether the three executives used cases on their own iPhone 4s. Jobs, Cook, and Mansfield each hold up an uncased phone. Jobs later gives a lengthy answer about what Apple learned from the controversy, including why the company waited to gather more data before holding the press conference.<br/>
<br/>
